=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk ticket's core claim — that Campus 1 PCs cannot reach Campus 2 (10.5.0.x / 10.6.0.x) and that router16's OSPF process has died — is entirely false. Every component of the network was inspected and found healthy.

**Router16 OSPF:** The FRR OSPF process on router16 is fully operational. It maintains two Full-state OSPF adjacencies: one with mswitch0 (172.16.30.254, Full/Backup via bond0) and one with router7 (192.168.10.1, Full/DR via eth2). Its running config correctly announces both connected prefixes (10.2.2.0/24 and 172.30.1.0/24) into area 0, and its FIB holds complete OSPF-learned routes to all subnets including 10.5.0.0/24 and 10.6.0.0/24.

**End-to-end data plane:** Campus 1 PCs from all three VLANs (VLAN 10 — pc5/172.16.10.5, VLAN 20 — pc9/172.16.20.9, VLAN 30 — pc12/172.16.30.12) were tested and all successfully reach Campus 2 hosts pc1 (10.5.0.1) and pc3 (10.6.0.3) with 0% packet loss. The full path — mswitch0 → router16 → router7 → router8/router10 → router17 → sshswitch → switch4 → end hosts — is functioning correctly. The one failed ping in initial testing targeted 10.6.0.1, which is simply a non-existent host (no device is configured at that address; pc3 is at 10.6.0.3), producing an expected ARP-miss unreachable from sshswitch, not a routing failure.

**Root cause:** There is no fault. The ticket is a false premise likely based on a previous outage that has since been resolved, or a misidentified address.

**Corrective action:** No change required; close the ticket as no-fault-found.
=== END ===